{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And Elohim said: Let us make man</b> — He singled out the making of man with a special utterance because of the greatness of his stature, for his nature is not like the nature of the living creature and the beast that He created in the utterance preceding it. And the correct plain sense of the word \"let us make\" is, because He has already shown you to know (Ramban on Genesis 1:1) that Elohim created something from nothing on the first day alone, and afterward, from those created elements, He formed and made; and as He gave the waters the power to swarm forth a living soul, and the utterance concerning them was \"Let the waters swarm\" (Genesis 1:20), and the utterance concerning the beast was \"Let the earth bring forth\" (Genesis 1:24), so concerning man He said \"Let us make,\" that is to say: I and the earth that has been mentioned shall make man — the earth shall bring forth the body from its elements as it did with the beast and the living creature, as it is written (Genesis 2:7), \"And YHWH Elohim formed the man, dust from the earth,\" and He, blessed be He, shall give the spirit from the mouth of the Most High, as it is written (ibid.), \"and He breathed into his nostrils the breath of life.\" And He said \"in our image, after our likeness,\" because he will resemble both of them: in the measure of his body to the earth from which he was taken, and he will resemble in spirit to the supernal beings, which are not body and do not die. And the second verse says, \"in the image of Elohim He created him\" (Genesis 1:27), to recount the wonder that was wrought in him beyond the rest of creation. And this plain sense of this verse I have found from Rabbi Yosef Kimchi, and it is the most apparent of all that they have thought concerning it. And the meaning of \"צֶלֶם\" (tzelem) is like form/appearance, as in \"וּצְלֵם אַנְפּוֹהִי אֶשְׁתַּנִּי\" — \"and the form of his face was changed\" (Daniel 3:19), and likewise \" Surely man walks in a mere image\" (צֶלֶם) (Psalms 39:7), \"in the city you despise their image\" (צֶלֶם) (Psalms 73:20) — the form of his appearance. And \"דְמוּת\" (demut) is resemblance in shape and in deed, for those who are close in matter are called resembling one another. And behold, man resembles the lower beings and the supernal beings in form and in majesty, as it is written (Psalms 8:6), \"and You crown him with glory and majesty\" — and it is the orientation of his face in wisdom and in knowledge and in skill of deed, and in actual likeness, that his body resembles dust and his soul the supernal beings." "<b>And rule over the fish of the sea</b> — Because he was male and female, He said \"and they shall rule\" (וְיִרְדּוּ) over the fish of the sea — in the plural. And in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 7:5) they said: \"Let the earth bring forth a living soul after its kind\" — Rabbi Elazar said: \"a living soul\" — this is the spirit of the first man. And it is not possible that Rabbi Elazar should say that \"let the earth bring forth\" should be interpreted as referring to the soul of the first man at all; rather, his intention is toward what I have mentioned, to say that the formation of man in his spirit — that is the soul which is in the blood — was made from the earth, like the living creature and the beast, for all the souls of movement were made together, and afterward He created bodies for them. He first made the bodies of the beast and the living creature, and afterward the body of man, and placed in it this soul, and afterward breathed into it a supernal soul (נְשָׁמָה). For the distinct soul that is in him is that on account of which He singled out another utterance, to Elohim who gave it, as it is written (Genesis 2:7), \"and He breathed into his nostrils the breath of life.\" And the way of truth in this verse will be known to the discerning from the second verse; and it is possible that Rabbi Elazar intended it, and interpreted \"let the earth bring forth\" as the land of the living, which brings forth a living soul after its kind, standing forever. And likewise what He said, \"male and female He created them\" (below v. 27; below 5:2) — for the creation was initially of male and female, and their soul (נְשָׁמָה) was included within them, but the formation (יְצִירָה) was a formation of the man and a construction of a rib for the woman, as He will recount at the end. And therefore He mentioned here \"creation\" (בְּרִיאָה) and in the section below He mentioned \"formation\" (יְצִירָה), and the discerning will understand. And the meaning of \"and they shall rule\" (וְיִרְדוּ) is that they shall rule with force over the fish and the fowl and the beast and every creeping thing, and \"the beast\" includes the wild animal (חַיָּה). And He said \"and over all the earth\" — that they shall rule over the earth itself: to uproot and to demolish and to dig and to hew copper and iron. And the expression רְדִיָּה denotes the dominion of a master over his slave."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {1 "In comment 1, the phrase 'to Elohim who gave it' (אל האלהים אשר נתנה) is rendered hyper-literally; the syntactic relationship between the clauses is textually difficult and may be corrupt."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-21T22:15:25.222670831Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}